# DTF tier manifest: token-repo-restriction
#   (artifact-keeper#4219, #4225, #4226, GHSA-2mfv-xg68-gq4p and
#    GHSA-gvhj-8vg9-358g; all shipped in 1.11.0, the last two via #4588)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. Every fix lives in the token mint
# handlers, the authentication middleware and the download-ticket store, ahead
# of any storage backend, so the oracle is pure HTTP plus direct DB reads (and
# one direct DB write that plants an unparseable stored selector).
#
# THE BUGS
#   #4219  POST /api/v1/auth/tokens had no repo_selector field and ignored
#          unknown fields, so the selector the web UI sends was dropped and the
#          personal token was minted unrestricted. Fixed: the selector is stored
#          (api_tokens.repo_selector) and enforced on every read, write and
#          listing path; an empty selector, a misspelled criterion and any
#          unknown field are 400.
#   #4225  A repository-restricted credential (personal repo_selector, or a
#          service-account token's repository_ids) could mint an unrestricted
#          sibling on /auth/tokens and /profile/access-tokens. Fixed: the new
#          token inherits the restriction as a match_repos selector, naming its
#          own selector is 403, and a restriction that matches nothing cannot
#          mint at all (403).
#   #4226  A stored repo_selector that did not parse was read as "no
#          restriction"; service-account token creation stored a selector as
#          given and accepted an empty repository_ids; the other mint endpoints
#          ignored unknown fields. Fixed: an unparseable stored selector grants
#          no repository, and every mint request refuses unknown fields and
#          non-restricting selectors with 400.
#   GHSA-2mfv  POST /auth/ticket stored only the user, so a redeemed ticket
#          carried the user's full reach, not the minting token's restriction.
#          This was latent because redemption itself was broken (the user
#          lookup's column list had drifted, so every ticket fell through to
#          anonymous). Fixed together: the ticket records the minter's
#          restriction (download_tickets.allowed_repo_ids, scope_recorded),
#          redemption works again and restores the restriction, and minting
#          needs the read:artifacts scope.
#   GHSA-gvhj  PUT /repositories/{key}/security checked the repository admin
#          action but not the token scope, so a read:artifacts-only token held
#          by a repository admin could switch scanning off. Fixed: the route
#          requires write:repositories (403 otherwise).
#
# THE FIXTURE
#   A, B   private generic repositories, each holding dir/obj.txt with distinct
#          bytes. U is a plain user granted read, write and admin on both, so
#          every denial below is the TOKEN's restriction, never U's reach.
#   TA     U's personal token, read+write:artifacts, repo_selector match_repos [A].
#   TU     U's personal token with no selector (the unrestricted control).
#   SR     a service-account token with repository_ids [A] (the restriction
#          mechanism that already worked before 1.11.0; the control).
#
# THE GATES
#   (FIX)      RED on 1.10.0 (the image before all five fixes), GREEN on 1.11.0
#              and later: every case named FIX in the oracle.
#   (CONTROL)  must hold on BOTH: TU and the U session read, write and list A
#              and B; SR reads A and not B; an unauthenticated mint is 401 and
#              a forged ticket or no credential reads none of the private bytes;
#              the U session and a write:repositories token may change the scan
#              configuration.
#
# The ticket cases tolerate the 1.10.0 shape on purpose: there every ticket is
# anonymous, so "a restricted ticket cannot read B" holds trivially. The FIX
# case therefore requires the SAME restricted token's ticket to read A, and an
# unrestricted ticket to read B, before it accepts the B refusal as the
# restriction at work.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: ~90 authenticated calls back to back.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
